"""Original differential/optimization transfer, with objective hard checks."""
def add_questions(add, questions):
    def put(lesson,suffix,title,level,prompt,answer,solution,follow,follow_solution,hints,rubric):
        add(f'q_{lesson}_{suffix}',title,'Calculus' if lesson=='l26' else 'Optimization',level,'researcher',{'Easy':6,'Medium':12,'Hard':25}[level],prompt,answer,hints,solution,follow,follow_solution,lesson)
        questions[-1]['rubric']=rubric
    put('l26','e1','A derivative has an inner factor','Easy',
        r'Let \(\ell(\theta)=\tfrac12(2\theta-3)^2\), for real θ. Compute \(\ell^{\prime}(1)\). Give the signed derivative, not a proposed update.',-2,
        r'Write r=2θ−3. The outer derivative is r and the inner derivative is 2, so \(\ell^{\prime}(\theta)=2(2\theta-3)\). At θ=1 this is −2. Increasing θ slightly lowers the loss. The factor one half cancels the outer factor two, but not the inner derivative.',
        'What is the exact loss change from θ=1 to θ=1+h?',r'Expanding gives \(\Delta\ell=-2h+2h^2\). The linear prediction is reliable locally; h=1 gives zero change, despite a negative derivative at the starting point.',
        ['Differentiate both the outer square and the inner affine function.', 'Evaluate the residual at θ=1 after applying the chain rule.'],['Retain the inner factor two.', 'Distinguish a signed slope from a finite update.'])
    put('l26','e2','Choose a direction, then take the dot product','Easy',
        r'Let \(f(x,y)=\tfrac12(x+2y-5)^2\). At (1,1), find the directional derivative along the UNIT vector \(u=(3/5,4/5)\), defined as the derivative at t=0 of f((1,1)+tu).',-22/5,
        r'The residual is −2, giving gradient (−2,−4). The directional derivative is \(\nabla f^Tu=(-2)(3/5)+(-4)(4/5)=-22/5\). Normalizing a direction matters: the parameter t measures distance because u has unit Euclidean norm.',
        'Which unit direction gives the most negative derivative?',r'The direction is \(-\nabla f/\|\nabla f\|=(1,2)/\sqrt5\), with derivative \(-\sqrt{20}\). Cauchy–Schwarz bounds every unit-direction dot product below by minus the gradient norm.',
        ['Compute the residual before the two partial derivatives.', 'A unit-direction derivative is the dot product with the gradient.'],['Compute the full gradient.', 'Use the stated unit vector and signed dot product.'])
    put('l26','m1','The same loss in different coordinates','Medium',
        r'For θ>0 define \(\ell(\theta)=\tfrac12(\theta-4)^2\). Set θ=e^β and F(β)=ℓ(e^β). Compute F′ at β=log 2.',-4,
        r'At θ=2 the original derivative is −2. The chain rule adds \(d\theta/d\beta=\theta=2\), so F′=−4. A derivative is a sensitivity per unit of its coordinate; these are two coordinate descriptions of one loss, not inconsistent calculations.',
        'Compare small gradient steps of size α in θ and in β, starting at θ=2.',r'The direct step is θnew=2+2α. The β step is βnew=log2+4α, hence θnew=2e^(4α)=2+8α+O(α²). Equal numerical learning rates in different coordinates are not the same movement. The transformed Hessian is θ²ℓ″+θℓ′; here it is zero, although ℓ″=1.',
        ['Differentiate the map θ=e^β as well as the loss.', 'At β=log 2, the coordinate-map derivative equals two.'],['Apply composition at the specified coordinate.', 'Interpret coordinate-dependent gradient updates.'])
    put('l26','m2','The axes conceal a saddle','Medium',
        r'For \(f(x,y)=x^2+4xy+y^2\), find the smallest directional second derivative at the origin over all UNIT Euclidean vectors u. Report its signed value.',-2,
        r'The Hessian is \(H=\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\4&2\end{pmatrix}\). For a unit vector, \(u^THu=2+8u_xu_y\). Since \(u_xu_y\ge-1/2\), the minimum is −2, attained at (1,−1)/√2. Along either coordinate axis the curvature is +2, yet along x=−y the function is negative. The origin is a saddle.',
        'What changes if the mixed term is 2xy instead of 4xy?',r'Then H has eigenvalues 0 and 4 and f=(x+y)². Every point on x=−y is a global minimum. Semidefinite curvature permits flat directions; positive definiteness would rule those out for this quadratic.',
        ['Write the mixed derivative twice in the symmetric Hessian.', 'Bound the product of the two unit-vector coordinates.'],['Find the least directional curvature.', 'Give its attaining direction and explain the missed saddle.'])
    put('l26','h1','Both partials exist, but the local plane fails','Hard',
        r'Define \(f(x,y)=xy/\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\) away from (0,0), and f(0,0)=0. Determine continuity, both coordinate partials, and total differentiability at the origin. Test the proposed linear approximation on x=y=t>0. Use the numerical and exact-choice checkpoints below; your scratchpad is ungraded.',None,
        r'For r=√(x²+y²), |xy|≤r²/2, so |f|≤r/2→0: f is continuous. On either axis f is zero, hence both coordinate partials are zero. If a total derivative existed, its linear map would therefore be zero. But at (t,t), t>0, f=t/√2 while the distance is √2t, so the normalized remainder is 1/2 for every t>0, not a quantity tending to zero. Total differentiability fails. On the unit diagonal, f(hu)=|h|/2, so even the two-sided directional derivative fails there. Existing partials alone do not justify the gradient approximation.',
        'Would squaring the numerator, f=x²y²/√(x²+y²), restore differentiability?',r'Yes. Its absolute value is at most r³/4. Dividing by r gives a remainder bound r²/4→0, proving total differentiability with zero derivative. A proof must control all approaches, not only a handful of paths.',
        ['Compute the axes, then the diagonal.', 'Divide the remainder by distance, not by distance squared.'],['Prove continuity with a uniform bound.', 'Evaluate the normalized diagonal remainder.', 'Distinguish partials and total differentiability.'])
    put('l26','h2','One parameter is used three times','Hard',
        r'A recurrence has x₀=2, \(x_t=a x_{t-1}+u_t\), t=1,2,3, with a=1/2 and inputs [1,0,−1]. All targets are zero. Let \(\ell=(x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2)/6\). Derive its derivative with respect to the SHARED a and with respect to x₀, holding inputs fixed. Select the valid reverse-derivative claims. These derivatives concern this finite computation, not a stationary distribution.',None,
        r'The states are 2,1,−1/2 and loss 7/8. Set λ₄=0 and \(\lambda_t=x_t/3+a\lambda_{t+1}\). Working backwards gives λ₃=−1/6, λ₂=1/4, λ₁=19/24. Each occurrence of a contributes λt times its input state, so \(d\ell/da=(19/24)2+(1/4)2+(-1/6)1=23/12\). Also \(d\ell/dx_0=a\lambda_1=19/48\), and derivatives with respect to the three inputs are λ₁,λ₂,λ₃. A forward check differentiates the recurrence: s₀=0, st=x(t−1)+a s(t−1), giving s=[2,3,5/2]; then Σxt st/3=23/12. Detaching states drops real paths through the computation.',
        'What happens if each time uses its own independent parameter aₜ?',r'The three separate derivatives are [19/12,1/2,−1/6]. The derivative for a shared a is their sum, because changing a changes every aₜ simultaneously. A negative final contribution can coexist with a positive total derivative. Reverse mode computes all input sensitivities in linear time.',
        ['Carry the future loss contribution backwards through a.', 'Sum the parameter contribution from every time index.'],['Normalize the direct residual by three.', 'Accumulate every shared-parameter use.', 'Cross-check with forward sensitivities.'])
    put('l27','e1','A stationary point with a certificate','Easy',
        r'Minimize \(f(x)=3x^2-12x+7\) over all real x. Report the unique minimizing x and verify that it is a global minimum.',2,
        r'The derivative is 6x−12, zero at x=2, and \(f=3(x-2)^2-5\). The nonnegative square proves that the minimum −5 is attained uniquely at 2. A zero derivative alone would not have been a global certificate.',
        'Does strict convexity always imply an attained minimum on the real line?',r'No: e^(−x) is strictly convex, with infimum zero as x→∞, but it never attains zero. This quadratic is also coercive: it grows without bound as |x| grows, ensuring existence.',
        ['Complete the square after finding the stationary point.', 'A positive squared term establishes a global lower bound.'],['Find the stationary point.', 'Certify the attained unique global minimum.'])
    put('l27','e2','The gradient points outside the box','Easy',
        r'Minimize \(f(x)=\tfrac12(x-3)^2\) over the closed interval [0,1]. Report its minimizing x.',1,
        r'The derivative x−3 is negative throughout the interval, so f decreases up to x=1. The minimizer is 1, with derivative −2. The suggested increase in x is infeasible. For every feasible z, f′(1)(z−1)=−2(z−1)≥0, a convex first-order certificate.',
        'Write the multiplier of the upper constraint x−1≤0.',r'The stationarity equation is f′(1)+μ=0, giving μ=2≥0. The lower constraint −x≤0 is inactive and has multiplier zero. The upper multiplier measures the first-order benefit of relaxing that bound.',
        ['Respect the interval before solving an unconstrained stationarity equation.', 'Check the sign of x−3 everywhere in the feasible interval.'],['Use monotonicity on the feasible interval.', 'Explain the nonzero boundary gradient.'])
    put('l27','m1','The stability endpoint is excluded','Medium',
        r'For \(f(x,y)=\tfrac12(2x^2+10y^2)\), use fixed-step gradient descent with α>0. Find the UPPER ENDPOINT of the interval of α for convergence to (0,0) from EVERY starting point. The endpoint itself is excluded; report the endpoint, not a largest admissible step.',1/5,
        r'Coordinates evolve as xnext=(1−2α)x and ynext=(1−10α)y. Both multipliers must have absolute value below one. Intersecting the intervals gives 0<α<1/5. At α=1/5 the y component alternates without shrinking. At α=1/4, starting at (1,1), the next state is (1/2,−3/2) and the loss rises from 6 to 23/2. A negative gradient direction is local information, not a guarantee for an arbitrary finite step.',
        'Find the best fixed step for the worst contraction across both coordinates.',r'Balance 1−2α=−(1−10α), obtaining α=1/6. The worst absolute multiplier is 2/3. This minimizes the maximum endpoint contraction; the squared loss contracts at most by (2/3)² per step.',
        ['Analyze both signed coordinate multipliers.', 'A multiplier with magnitude exactly one does not shrink.'],['Give the open stability interval.', 'Distinguish endpoint oscillation from convergence.'])
    put('l27','m2','Backtrack for sufficient decrease','Medium',
        r'Let \(f(z)=\tfrac12 z^THz\), \(H=\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\), and z=(1,1). With g=∇f(z), try z−αg. Start α=1, halving until \(f(z-\alpha g)\le f(z)-\tfrac14\alpha\|g\|^2\). Report the first accepted α; equality is accepted.',1/4,
        r'Here g=(5,3), ||g||²=34 and gᵀHg=148. Exact expansion gives fnew=4−34α+74α². Armijo requires α≤51/148. Steps 1 and 1/2 fail; 1/4 passes, after two halvings. The candidate is (−1/4,1/4), with loss 1/8, below the Armijo bound 15/8. This is a decrease check, not a claim that the candidate is optimal.',
        'What should an implementation return when g=0?',r'There is no descent direction to search. Under the exercise convention return the unchanged point, step zero and zero halvings. For a general nonconvex function, g=0 alone does not certify a minimum.',
        ['Expand the quadratic along −g exactly.', 'Keep the Armijo coefficient one quarter distinct from the step.'],['Compute the gradient and directional curvature.', 'Find the first passing dyadic step.'])
    put('l27','h1','An active constraint can have zero multiplier','Hard',
        r'Minimize \(f(x,y)=\tfrac12(x^2+4y^2)\) subject to x+y=1 and y≥a, where 0≤a≤1. Derive the full solution as a function of a, then give the minimum value and inequality multiplier at a=1/2. Use \(\mathcal L=f+\nu(x+y-1)+\mu(a-y)\), μ≥0. Classify what happens at a=1/5 and justify global uniqueness.',None,
        r'Substitute x=1−y: f=1/2−y+(5/2)y², with derivative 5y−1. Thus y*=max(a,1/5), x*=1−y*. The strictly positive reduced curvature gives a unique global minimum. Stationarity gives ν=−x*, μ=4y*+ν=5y*−1; below the threshold μ=0. At a=1/2, x=y=1/2, value 5/8, μ=3/2 and ν=−1/2. At a=1/5 the inequality is tight but μ=0. Complementarity requires a product to vanish, not a positive multiplier whenever a constraint is tight. For a>1/5 the derivative of the optimal value with respect to a is 5a−1=μ.',
        'Can KKT necessity fail even at an attained constrained optimum?',r'Yes. Minimize x subject to x²≤0. The only feasible point is x=0, but stationarity would be 1+μ·0=0, impossible. A constraint qualification is needed for general necessity. This does not invalidate convex KKT sufficiency when a valid feasible certificate with the correct signs is available.',
        ['Eliminate the equality first.', 'Separate the inactive and tight regimes.'],['Derive both solution regimes.', 'Respect the Lagrangian signs and equality multiplier.', 'Explain tightness without a positive multiplier.'])
    put('l27','h2','Clipping solves the wrong geometry','Hard',
        r'Let \(f(x,y)=\tfrac12(x^2+2xy+2y^2)-2x-3y\) on \([0,2]\times[0,1/2]\). The unconstrained minimum is (1,1). A colleague clips it to (1,1/2). Find the true constrained minimizer and quantify the loss gap of clipping. Give the optimum x coordinate and nonnegative loss gap in the numerical checks; select valid certificates.',None,
        r'At the clipped point the gradient is (−1/2,−1), so a feasible increase in x reduces loss. Holding y=1/2, minimize in x: x+y−2=0 gives x=3/2. At (3/2,1/2), the gradient is (0,−1/2). For any feasible displacement δ, δy≤0, so gᵀδ≥0. The Hessian has leading entry 1 and determinant 1, hence is positive definite. Exact expansion f(z+δ)−f(z)=gᵀδ+δᵀHδ/2 proves strict global uniqueness. The clipped value is −9/4, optimal value −19/8, gap 1/8. Completing the square shows that the constrained problem projects (1,1) in the H-induced metric, not ordinary Euclidean distance.',
        'Describe an exact algorithm for any positive-definite two-variable quadratic on a closed box, including degenerate boxes.',r'Include an unconstrained solution if feasible. On each of the four faces, fix one coordinate and minimize the one-dimensional positive-curvature quadratic in the other, clamping that scalar optimum to its allowed interval. Compare their objective values. Face minima include corners, and zero-width bounds are allowed. Any boundary optimum lies on a face; a feasible interior optimum is the stationary solution. This exhausts candidates in dimension two and is not a general high-dimensional QP algorithm.',
        ['Check feasible descent at the clipped candidate.', 'Use a first-order inequality plus positive curvature for a global certificate.'],['Find the free-coordinate optimum on the active face.', 'Compute and sign the clipping gap.', 'Certify all feasible displacements, not only axes.'])


def objective_checks():
    # Lazy import keeps coding-bank construction free of registry side effects.
    from automatic_assessments import N,S
    return {
      'q_l26_h1': [N('For t>0, what is f(t,t) divided by the Euclidean distance from (t,t) to the origin?',.5,'The numerator is t/√2 and the distance is √2t.'), S('Select every valid conclusion about the original f.',
       (True,'The function is continuous at the origin.','Its absolute value is bounded by half the distance.'),
       (True,'Both coordinate partial derivatives at the origin are zero.','The function vanishes along both axes.'),
       (False,'A zero gradient of partials proves total differentiability here.','The diagonal remainder divided by distance stays at one half.'),
       (False,'The two-sided directional derivative along the unit diagonal exists.','Along that line the function is the absolute value of the line parameter divided by two.'),
       (True,'If a total derivative existed, its linear map would be zero.','A total derivative must reproduce the two coordinate partials.'))],
      'q_l26_h2': [N('What is dℓ/da for the shared parameter?',23/12,'Sum every parameter-use contribution, not just the final direct one.'),N('What is dℓ/dx₀?',19/48,'Multiply the first adjoint by a.'),S('Select every valid differentiation rule for this recurrence.',
       (True,'Each input derivative equals the adjoint at its time index.','The local input derivative of the state is one.'),
       (True,'The shared-parameter derivative sums contributions from all three occurrences.','One perturbation of a changes every occurrence.'),
       (False,'Only the final state contributes to the shared-parameter derivative.','Earlier losses and intermediate pathways also depend on a.'),
       (False,'Detach intermediate states and the exact shared derivative is unchanged.','Detachment deletes causal differentiation paths.'),
       (True,'The backwards adjoint adds the current residual divided by three to a times the next adjoint.','The loss uses the mean half-square and the next state has derivative a.'))],
      'q_l27_h1': [N('What is the minimum objective at a=1/2?',5/8,'Substitute x=y=one half.'),N('What is μ at a=1/2 under the stated sign convention?',1.5,'Stationarity gives μ=5y minus one.'), S('Select every valid conclusion for the full parameterized problem.',
       (True,'Below a=1/5, the inequality is slack and its multiplier is zero.','The unconstrained reduced minimizer remains feasible.'),
       (True,'At a=1/5, the inequality is tight with zero multiplier.','Complementarity allows this degeneracy.'),
       (False,'The equality multiplier must be nonnegative.','Equality multipliers have unrestricted sign.'),
       (False,'Stationarity always proves a global minimum for any smooth loss.','Global certification here uses convexity and feasibility.'),
       (True,'The positive reduced curvature makes the feasible minimizer unique.','The objective restricted to the equality line is strictly convex.'))],
      'q_l27_h2': [N('What is the x coordinate of the true constrained minimizer?',1.5,'Optimize the free coordinate on the top face.'),N('What is the loss at the clipped point minus the optimal loss?',1/8,'Minus nine quarters minus minus nineteen eighths is one eighth.'),S('Select every valid explanation or certificate.',
       (True,'Increasing x slightly from the clipped point is feasible and lowers the loss.','Its x derivative is negative one half.'),
       (True,'At the correct optimum, the x derivative is zero and the y derivative is negative one half.','The free coordinate is stationary; descent in y points outside the box.'),
       (False,'The nonzero y derivative rules out constrained optimality.','No feasible positive y movement is available.'),
       (False,'Euclidean clipping of an unconstrained minimum always minimizes a coupled quadratic.','Cross curvature changes the metric and the free coordinate.'),
       (True,'The positive-definite Hessian and the feasible first-order inequality certify a unique global optimum.','The exact quadratic remainder is strictly positive for nonzero feasible displacement.'))]}
